.Dd November 9, 2026
.Dt KCMP 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kcmp
.Nd whether two processes share a kernel object
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In linux/kcmp.h
.In sys/syscall.h
.In unistd.h
.Ft int
.Fn syscall SYS_kcmp "pid_t pid1" "pid_t pid2" "int type" "unsigned long idx1" "unsigned long idx2"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn kcmp
tells whether the processes or threads
.Fa pid1
and
.Fa pid2 ,
numbered in the caller's PID namespace, hold the same kernel object of the
kind
.Fa type ,
as Linux's call does.
Mesa asks it of two descriptors of DRM nodes, to learn whether they are one
open file.
.Bl -tag -width "KCMP_EPOLL_TFD"
.It Dv KCMP_FILE
The open file descriptions of descriptor
.Fa idx1
of
.Fa pid1
and descriptor
.Fa idx2
of
.Fa pid2 .
A
.Xr dup 2 ,
a descriptor a
.Xr fork 2
copied, and one passed over a socket are the same; two
.Xr open 2
calls of one file are not.
.It Dv KCMP_VM
The address spaces, shared by threads and by
.Dv CLONE_VM .
.It Dv KCMP_FILES
The descriptor tables, shared by threads and by
.Dv CLONE_FILES .
.It Dv KCMP_FS
The working and root directories with the umask, shared by threads and by
.Dv CLONE_FS .
.It Dv KCMP_SIGHAND
The signal dispositions, which a thread group shares.
.It Dv KCMP_IO
The I/O contexts.
A process has none until
.Xr ioprio_set 2
sets its priority, and then one of its own, since no call shares one; two
processes without are the same.
.It Dv KCMP_SYSVSEM
The System V semaphore adjustments
.Dv SEM_UNDO
made, which a thread group shares.
A process has none until its first such
.Xr semop 2 ;
two processes without are the same.
.It Dv KCMP_EPOLL_TFD
The open file of descriptor
.Fa idx1
of
.Fa pid1
against the file registered in an epoll set of
.Fa pid2 .
.Fa idx2
is the address of a
.Vt struct kcmp_epoll_slot ,
whose
.Va efd
is the set's descriptor in
.Fa pid2 ,
.Va tfd
the descriptor number registered and
.Va toff
which of the registrations of that number, from 0.
.El
.Pp
The caller must be allowed to read both processes as
.Xr ptrace 2
allows it: its own thread group, a privileged caller, or one whose real
user and group IDs are each of the target's three, with the target
dumpable and not privileged.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
0 when the objects are the same.
Otherwise 1 or 2, by the order of the objects' addresses under a key drawn
at random once per boot: 1 when the first is below the second, 2 when it
is above.
The order is the same for every call of a boot, so a program may sort
objects by it, and tells nothing of the addresses.
On failure -1 is returned with
.Va errno
set; the kernel returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Pa tests/host/drm_core_hosttest.c
compares a
.Xr dup 2
of a DRM node's descriptor, a forked child's copy and two opens of the node.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[ESRCH]"
.It Bq Er EBADF
A descriptor named is not open, or for
.Dv KCMP_EPOLL_TFD
the set's descriptor is not.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
The
.Vt struct kcmp_epoll_slot
cannot be read.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa type
is none of the above, or the
.Va efd
of
.Dv KCMP_EPOLL_TFD
is no epoll set.
.It Bq Er ENOENT
The epoll set has no such registration.
.It Bq Er EPERM
The caller may not read one of the processes.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
.Fa pid1
or
.Fa pid2
names no process or thread the caller can see.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dup 2 ,
.Xr epoll 2 ,
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr ptrace 2 ,
.Xr drm 4
